#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71541ac7-8988-5aa5-9dcf-893226b0bdd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-10$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281603} {:answer/id #uuid "343d1790-9290-574e-aea2-cddafbb5cf00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-10,$ $x=-5,$  and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281604} {:answer/id #uuid "abea6dbd-cbc4-5e27-a20a-3c43e9c4070a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-10$ and $x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281605} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f653f3-165c-5e22-b98b-66497534353a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281606} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7a9e75-e0dd-5c1a-a5a5-6d6160328651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abea6dbd-cbc4-5e27-a20a-3c43e9c4070a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-10$ and $x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281605} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1a74be8-16fd-5929-85ad-92fc12e791c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b49effd-34ee-539b-94bb-4a761d8bf290" :question/math-academy-id "q-101991" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/assets/6ec1bb6f164aa4878480f06707722769d1e17ff735a04c29a3fc2b9bd09ca467.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-10$ the function has a corner, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=-5$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-10$ and $x=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c24a4c5-5774-5e61-9500-ac29447b5de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281610} {:answer/id #uuid "bda7d1bf-2be3-55d6-9338-f0cc39fd2b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281611} {:answer/id #uuid "86b056ee-6fd7-5a9b-90d6-a4641b906f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281612} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc1c01b-0fec-517d-99c1-76388bbf37c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281613} {:answer/id #uuid "2b9df08e-5be5-561b-987c-e879133c9de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bda7d1bf-2be3-55d6-9338-f0cc39fd2b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281611} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5b356e-1847-5521-9d23-2557c1554c47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7c7ee1b-da55-5996-923f-38ff8f88ec23" :question/math-academy-id "q-102163" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-2, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true  Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, at  $x=0.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x-2)=2(0)-2=-2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}-2=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=f(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ .\n\n- Statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\,$  if  $\\,x<0$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>0$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$   Since $x=0$  doesn't belong to $(0,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814a3015-040d-5d38-8d89-66e613ea9c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$, $x = 0$, and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114136} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc4ca4a-1ffc-505e-9c15-eada8368fb5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114137} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f356c3-5dd2-587a-aa58-7ab4bc07f2f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114138} {:answer/id #uuid "cd84e69a-febf-59d0-8ce5-b1b9373d8954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114139} {:answer/id #uuid "551564d0-36a1-5e6f-a7f1-ebd7c47f4f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ and $x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "814a3015-040d-5d38-8d89-66e613ea9c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$, $x = 0$, and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114136} :answer-field/id #uuid "3caeafb3-6b65-5977-a595-ae7d734d711e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "097f3e11-2ce8-55f7-bbfa-1640ee282b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-47650" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/assets/8e7daf95fadbe2262a09e8183f00d63075655f3b97dd363984c192ac3414ad17.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db442a7d-d3e2-529a-9d0a-6c69508a1724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281616} {:answer/id #uuid "efe17991-0589-557d-933d-2c09e635dbf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{x-3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281617} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b1841a-1768-51da-a465-19b5a0c1181d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=3|x-3|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281618} {:answer/id #uuid "9b585a37-6598-5862-aed2-90725e72015a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-3, & x<3 \\\\ x+3, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281619} {:answer/id #uuid "e398c7b3-8f1d-5e55-b5ce-3ab659a86fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(x)=\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e398c7b3-8f1d-5e55-b5ce-3ab659a86fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(x)=\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281620} :answer-field/id #uuid "c597ab9b-1332-5810-9c9a-1684379ac297" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7b7d715-db4a-589d-9eb7-1419afc4759a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47678" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=3?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $p(x)$ is differentiable for all $x>0.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $p(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nOther functions are not differentiable at $x=3.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=3$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=3$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at  $x=3$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=3$, so it is not differentiable there."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b337561-3369-5032-a520-339b3b5258f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281623} {:answer/id #uuid "264d0e95-f1a0-5c15-a528-b2d04258ed30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281624} {:answer/id #uuid "43c3142b-6f4c-55ce-a2c0-dcf54fb3ed94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281625} {:answer/id #uuid "66cee65a-e733-517d-a359-982f12975856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281626} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7b7c32-db80-568a-8727-76265d71e313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b337561-3369-5032-a520-339b3b5258f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281623} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6654a34-2ccb-5cb7-a904-8088f96525ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8948446-9f58-5a7d-8b94-5971514bef07" :question/math-academy-id "q-51067" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$, has a cusp at $x=1,$ and is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=-2$\n- It's **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x+2|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a cusp at $x=1$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$"}]}
